{"entries":[{"name":"赤シャツ","reading":"あかシャツ","category":"character","keys":["赤シャツ","教頭"],"content":"Akashatsu is a vice-principal (教頭) and a 文学士 (Bachelor of Arts) who speaks in an oddly gentle, feminine voice. He wears a flannel undershirt even in the hot weather and wears red shirts all year round, claiming it is good for his health. The narrator finds his clothing ridiculous and feels that it mocks other people. He invites the narrator fishing, laughing with a loud ホホホホ, and he assumes he has won the argument over the morality of fishing. On the boat trip he admires the pine trees and the scenery, and he objects when the narrator asks to land on the island. When the narrator says he will confront Horita, Akashatsu becomes alarmed and warns him against violence, which the narrator sees as an attempt to protect himself."},{"name":"堀田","reading":"ほった","category":"character","keys":["堀田","堀田君","山嵐"],"content":"Horita is a mathematics teacher at the school, a sturdy man with a close-cropped head and a fierce, monk-like face. When the narrator politely showed him his appointment letter, he ignored it and laughed, inviting the narrator to visit, which the narrator found rude. The narrator gave him the nickname 山嵐. The narrator later tells Akashatsu that he intends to negotiate with Horita, and Akashatsu is greatly alarmed by this."},{"name":"吉川","reading":"よしかわ","category":"character","keys":["吉川","吉川君","野だ","野だいこ","画学の教師"],"content":"Yoshikawa is the art (drawing) teacher at the school, nicknamed 野だいこ by the narrator. He is theatrical, wearing a flimsy silk haori and fanning himself, and claims to be an Edokko when he learns the narrator is from Tokyo. He follows Akashatsu everywhere like a subordinate, and he accompanies Akashatsu when Akashatsu invites the narrator fishing. On the boat he claims that fishing needs no rod, only line, and he flatters Akashatsu's views on the scenery, even proposing to name the island Turner Island (ターナー島) after the painter. He also makes a suggestive remark about Madonna, which Akashatsu quickly stops."},{"name":"青嶋","reading":"あおしま","category":"place","keys":["青嶋"],"content":"Aoshima is an uninhabited island visible from the boat during the fishing trip, covered only with rocks and pine trees. Akashatsu praises the view from the water, and Yoshikawa calls it magnificent. The narrator asks whether the boat can land on the rocky part of the island, but Akashatsu objects that the shore is not suitable for fishing. Yoshikawa jokingly proposes renaming it Turner Island, which Akashatsu approves."},{"name":"マドンナ","reading":"まどんな","category":"term","keys":["マドンナ"],"content":"Madonna is a name that Yoshikawa and Akashatsu mention during the boat trip, with Yoshikawa suggesting a painting of Raphael's Madonna placed on the island. Akashatsu quickly changes the subject with a strange laugh. The narrator does not understand who she is and guesses that the name is a nickname for a geisha Akashatsu is fond of."}]}
