{"entries":[{"name":"ジョバンニ","reading":"じょばんに","category":"character","keys":["ジョバンニ","ジョバンニさん"],"content":"Giovanni is a schoolboy in class at the afternoon lesson. When the teacher asks about the Milky Way, he raises his hand but cannot answer clearly, and he blushes as Zanelli laughs at him. He remembers reading about the stars with Campanella at Campanella's house, and he feels sad because he has been tired from hard work, less lively at school, and talking less with his friends."},{"name":"カムパネルラ","reading":"かむぱねるら","category":"character","keys":["カムパネルラ","カムパネルラさん"],"content":"Campanella is a schoolboy in Giovanni's class who readily raises his hand to answer the teacher's question about the Milky Way. When called on, he stands but cannot answer. He is Giovanni's close friend, and the two once looked at a large book about the Milky Way together in the study of Campanella's father, a doctor. Giovanni suspects Campanella did not answer right away out of kindness, to spare Giovanni's feelings."},{"name":"ザネリ","reading":"ざねり","category":"character","keys":["ザネリ"],"content":"Zanelli is a classmate who sits in the front seat. When Giovanni cannot answer the teacher's question, Zanelli turns around and smiles at him, which makes Giovanni embarrassed."},{"name":"銀河","reading":"ぎんが","category":"term","keys":["銀河","天の川","銀河帯"],"content":"The Milky Way, shown as a pale, cloudy band across a large black star chart hung in the classroom. People have called it a river or a stream of milk, but the teacher explains that through a large telescope it appears as many small stars. The teacher uses a model with glowing grains in a lens to explain that we live inside this stream of stars, and that distant parts look dense and faintly white."}]}
