{"entries":[{"name":"宮本武蔵","reading":"みやもとむさし","category":"character","keys":["武蔵","武やん"],"content":"Takezō (武蔵), seventeen years old, lies among the dead on the battlefield at Sekigahara after the army he served has been defeated. He is wounded, with bullets likely lodged in his body, and is too weak to move. He left his village with his friend Matahachi against the wishes of his mother, his fiancée Otsū, and Matahachi's family, hoping to win glory as a soldier. Since joining the Shinmen household's forces, he has mostly been assigned menial tasks such as cutting grass rather than fighting."},{"name":"又八","reading":"またはち","category":"character","keys":["又八","又やん"],"content":"Matahachi (又八) is seventeen years old and from the same village as Takezō, and the two left home together to join the Shinmen household's army. He is also on the battlefield at Sekigahara, lying among the dead, and calls out to Takezō when he sees him move. He is the fiancé of Otsū (お通), who is a year younger than him. He and Takezō both feel their lives have been wasted, and Matahachi now wishes to return home."},{"name":"お通","reading":"おつう","category":"character","keys":["お通","お通さん"],"content":"Otsū (お通) is a young orphan raised at the Shippōji temple, and she is sixteen years old. She is the fiancée of Matahachi and is also connected to Takezō, who is her betrothed as well as Matahachi's friend; both men were pressed by her and their families to stay home. She is cold and lonely in manner, like the ash of an incense burner, and is waiting in the village while the men are away at war."},{"name":"金吾中納言秀秋","reading":"きんごちゅうなごんひであき","category":"character","keys":["金吾中納言秀秋","金吾中納言","金吾"],"content":"Kingo Chūnagon Hideaki (金吾中納言秀秋) secretly switched sides during the battle and attacked the western army together with the eastern army, which turned the battle and caused the defeat of the forces opposing the eastern side. Takezō blames him for the defeat and considers him a traitor."},{"name":"石田三成","reading":"いしだみつなり","category":"character","keys":["石田三成"],"content":"Ishida Mitsunari (石田三成) led the army that was defeated in the battle at Sekigahara. His forces, along with those of Ukita, Shimazu and Konishi, were overwhelmed after Kobayakawa Hideaki turned against them."},{"name":"関ヶ原","reading":"せきがはら","category":"place","keys":["関ヶ原","関ヶ原地方"],"content":"Sekigahara (関ヶ原) is the area where a great battle was fought in the ninth month of the fifth year of Keichō, with heavy rain falling on the battlefield the night before. Mount Ibuki and the Mino mountain ranges lie nearby. The battle was lost by the army Takezō served, and the field is strewn with the dead."},{"name":"七宝寺","reading":"しっぽうじ","category":"place","keys":["七宝寺"],"content":"Shippōji (七宝寺) is a temple in Takezō and Matahachi's village where Otsū lives and was raised as an orphan. Its veranda overlooks the rooftops of the village, and Otsū sits there watching the clouds while waiting for the men to return."},{"name":"新免家","reading":"しんめんけ","category":"organization","keys":["新免家","新免家の陣場"],"content":"The Shinmen household (新免家) is the lord whose army Takezō and Matahachi joined as volunteers. Although Takezō and Matahachi once served this household, it would not give them samurai rank, so they fought as lowly volunteers and were used mainly for labor such as scouting and cutting grass."}]}
